  <div class="shell">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="border-b border-stone-200 pb-2.5 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900">
        Understanding Immigration Law in California
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-3.5 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
        <p>
          Immigration law is federal, but how it plays out depends on the forum. Immigration judges
          hear removal cases, USCIS decides most applications and petitions, and federal courts
          review detention and certain agency actions. Knowing which of the three is handling your
          matter is the first step toward choosing the right relief.
        </p>
        <p>
          People in removal proceedings may be eligible for asylum, withholding of removal,
          protection under the Convention Against Torture, cancellation of removal or adjustment of
          status, depending on their facts. Family-based petitions require a qualifying
          relationship, proof of that relationship and eligibility to receive a green card. People
          held in immigration detention have due process rights, and a federal court can review
          whether the detention is lawful.
        </p>
        <p class="pt-1 text-[11px] text-slate-500 italic">
          This page is general information, not legal advice, and reading it does not create an
          attorney-client relationship. Immigration rules change often, so speak with an attorney
          about your own situation. Prior results do not guarantee a similar outcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
